WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.800
Film Courage: So on a movie like A CHILD’S
VOICE, where do you spend the most money?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:11.090
John Paul Rice, Film Producer: Well cast,
crew and all the logistics, locations and

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:18.539
food and equipment and insurance.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:26.369
Like it goes into the personnel, it’s all
the below-the-line, but not for us.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.380
It’s a labor of love at that point.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:40.230
Our stuff is not to pay ourselves (that’s
just me) but all of it goes into the planning,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:48.830
the implementation and also what I try to
do is look at the budget every couple of days

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:54.800
and start to try to figure out where I can
get donations, where I can get discounts,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.390
where I can smartly schedule things so that
we’re not spending double money to get the

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:09.740
same thing that we could if we plan it better
or smartly write it in a way that doesn’t

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.700
require…because you’ve just got to look
at what you’re trying to say.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.590
What are you trying to say with this scene
(with each scene)?

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:22.100
Do we really need to stretch it out or can
we consolidate here (maybe combine something).

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.843
So it’s a team effort between me and Edgar
[Edgar Michael Bravo].

00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:29.530
But mine are always to try to find things
that are reducing cost and overhead every

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:30.530
single time.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.190
Film Courage: Well you had the hotel [that
they shot scenes in for A CHILD’S VOICE].

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.560
Was that somewhere in Riverside [CA]?

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.560
John: It was in Riverside.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.000
Film Courage: Okay so it would be cheaper
than somewhere in LA I’m sure?

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.950
John: Right and Riverside is cheaper to film
and you still have to do all the permits and

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:41.950
everything.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.450
You still have to do everything else but it’s
not expensive.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.710
It’s gone up in the last couple of years
because they have more filming out there now.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.370
But the county of Riverside is fantastic.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.300
Those two women who run it are fantastic.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.100
As long as they’re there they will do really,
really well.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.710
The City of Riverside is independent of that
but the County of Riverside opens you up to

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.260
so many other things.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.760
They have location service that they will
help you with.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:17.379
We went to seven or eight hotels in different
groups and I drove out there and I lobbied

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:18.379
all of them.

00:02:18.379 --> 00:02:20.450
Film Courage: What’s that like?

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.799
John: It’s difficult.

00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:28.629
You’re going to meet a lot of people who
don’t care but some people when you talk

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:33.329
to them and you tell them what you’re doing
and what you’re about and the realities

00:02:33.329 --> 00:02:37.290
of what you’re dealing with…I’m dealing
with a crew of eight people, this many actors

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:43.819
coming in, I need this kind of room, can you
help us out?

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:49.049
We had help, we would not have been able to
make the film if we had not taken out more

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:55.180
money and away from something else potentially
where I would have to raise some later if

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:01.879
it wasn’t for that manager at that particular
hotel who gave us a discount because he was

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:08.170
personally invested in that issue and you
never know how things are going to come.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.870
But that came from the relationship with his
brother who knew the person who had endorsed

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.590
their previous film in a letter of writing
just so happen he knew who he was.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.010
Told these guys “Yeah, whatever they’re
doing if they’re backed by this guy, you

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.299
help them out whatever you can.”

00:03:22.299 --> 00:03:29.829
But it was who he was (the owner) and the
heart that he had for this issue.

00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:34.269
He came all the way out from Riverside with
his family and his mother and father to the

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:43.390
premiere and continued to help us afterward
by taking us into this hotel association (Asian

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:48.650
American Hotel Owners Association) no one
knows about this, 18,000 members, one out

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.159
of every two hotels in the country.

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:55.739
And one of their core tenants of what they’re
fighting against is human trafficking in their

00:03:55.739 --> 00:04:00.449
chains and they have a program (this is like
a top program) where all of their members

00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:05.120
have to receive training from the tops down
all the way housemaids on how to correctly

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.239
identify the signs of, how to document it
and how to get local law enforcement and local

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:16.280
government services involved to get it out
of there and still help them.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.989
Because the life is in danger once they know
that they’re there trafficking in that…because

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:25.409
they may go somewhere else but they may do
something else, more crime may come in.

00:04:25.409 --> 00:04:32.630
So these are the things when you connect with
people, because of what you’re driven by,

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:38.069
you speak truthfully to that and I saw a couple
people that no matter what I said to them

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:39.069
I just saw…

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:40.190
Film Courage: The lights were out?

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.199
John: They were like “Uh huh.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:43.199
Sure.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:44.199
Okay.

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:45.199
We’ll talk.”

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:46.199
And you don’t hear anything else.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.979
So it’s like a day or two or three that
I had to go out there, talk and meet people

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:52.259
and leave cards and follow-up.

00:04:52.259 --> 00:05:01.710
But but you’re talking about a hundred something
dollars a night per room versus less which

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:08.560
enables you do more with your budget and your
crew and maybe add a day or whatever or you

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.330
don’t have to worry about “How the hell
am I going to pay for this?”

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:16.199
It’s really valuable to go out there as
a producer that’s what I love to do.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:17.289
I’m a hands on guy.

00:05:17.289 --> 00:05:22.520
I like to get involved with everything, the
locations, all of it.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.271
Some people try to…there’s this one guy
Mark Stoloraff (I love the guy) he’s like

00:05:26.271 --> 00:05:29.560
“Beg, Barter and Steal.”

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.560
Right?

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.830
And to me it’s like “Okay, well I’d
rather not steal.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:35.800
And I don’t want to beg.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.889
But I definitely want to align myself with
people who like what we do.

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:40.889
Film Courage: Right.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:44.650
Like a Grateful Dead concert “Hey man, you
can have my spaghetti if I can get this.”

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:45.650
John: Exactly!

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:52.250
Film Courage: The thing is I bet you can probably
see in the ones that do show some signs of

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.880
life like this person is going to be easy
to work with?

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.659
Whereas with others it’s all about the dollar
signs you can just feel that…”Hey we’re

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:02.750
going to need to run an hour late?”

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.550
Was that some of what you were dealing with,
gauging their willingness?

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.050
John: Yeah because if this was the starting
point right?

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:14.389
This was what I was mentioning earlier about
being fixated on a person or an idea or a

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:19.949
way that you want to do it you just have to
adapt and be like “That’s not who they

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:25.419
are” and save yourself the trouble because
anything after that you’re going to have

00:06:25.419 --> 00:06:30.199
to be asking them permission to do all sorts
of things and what you want to do is (not

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:33.080
to hide if from them) but you want to partner.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.340
You want somebody who understands like when
we wanted to write that additional scene (the

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.340
pool scene)?

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.150
Film Courage: The one at night?

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.520
John: We came up with that while we were filming
and he said “Yeah, go for it!”

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.889
We didn’t have to sit there and negotiate
something with them.

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:48.889
He’s like “You guys do whatever you need
to do.”

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:49.889
We didn’t have to sit there and negotiate
something with them.

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:51.909
They were just like “Yeah, you guys do whatever
you need to do.”

00:06:51.909 --> 00:06:52.909
They loved us.

00:06:52.909 --> 00:06:55.419
They loved seeing the film.

00:06:55.419 --> 00:06:57.730
We brought them into the room and let the
watch the filming.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:06:59.460
Of course they get bored after 20 minutes.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.819
That’s how everybody does.

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:01.819
They are like “Oh?

00:07:01.819 --> 00:07:02.819
This is it?”

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:04.690
“Yeah, this is it.”

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.199
But making them a part of it and showing them
appreciation.

00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:15.789
We put their name in the credits as a very
special thanks and it’s just as a gesture.

00:07:15.789 --> 00:07:19.610
And you put a gesture out and someone will
give you a gesture back.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:25.840
It may not be everything that you want or
envisioned for your movie but work with those

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.930
people because they’re going to be there
when things don’t go right the way you planned

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:35.159
it and you may need something else and you
may need something else that you didn’t

00:07:35.159 --> 00:07:42.229
anticipate or something that comes up and
they were just so giving with us.

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:43.569
It was really a great thing.

